"""Guided lab notes and the course's conceptual-only Chapter 9 overview."""
from answers import p,ol,table,chart

def ex(title,q,a):return '<section class="worked-example"><div class="problem-statement"><h3>'+title+'</h3>'+q+'</div><div class="solution-panel" data-solution-panel style="color:#ffffff;background-color:#ffffff">'+a+'</div></section>'
PAGES={}
PAGES['1.5']='<h2>Data collection experiment</h2>'+p('Follow the textbook’s movie-survey sequence: collect observations, select respondents systematically, organize frequencies, and compare groupings. Keep class data separate from the illustrative data below.')
PAGES['1.5']+=ex('1. Collect and select',p('Each student asks five classmates from another class how many movies they saw at a theater last month. Explain how to select 12 respondents systematically from an ordered class list, then combine their five observations each. What could go wrong?'),p('Select a random start and use a fixed interval chosen to give 12 distinct respondents. The textbook uses a step of four; this works naturally with 48 names. With other list sizes, choose an appropriate interval and check for duplicates rather than repeatedly cycling through the same names. Twelve respondents × five observations = 60 values. Convenience recruitment, repeated people, and ordering patterns can introduce bias; systematic selection at the second stage does not fix first-stage bias.'))
counts=[5,10,15,12,8,5,3,2]
PAGES['1.5']+=ex('2. Build two frequency tables',p('For a worked illustration, use frequencies 5,10,15,12,8,5,3,2 for 0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7-or-more movies. Construct a frequency, relative-frequency, and cumulative-relative-frequency table. Then combine categories into 0–1, 2–3, 4–5, and 6-or-more. Repeat using your class data.'),table(['Movies','Frequency','Relative frequency','Cumulative'],[(str(k) if k<7 else '7+',v,f'{v/60:.4f}',f'{sum(counts[:k+1])/60:.4f}') for k,v in enumerate(counts)])+table(['Movies','Frequency','Relative frequency','Cumulative'],[('0–1',15,.25,.25),('2–3',27,.45,.70),('4–5',13,'0.2167','0.9167'),('6+',5,'0.0833',1)])+p('Each table has total frequency 60 and total relative frequency 1. Small rounding differences are acceptable.'))
PAGES['1.5']+=ex('3. Read percentages',ol('What percent saw at most two movies?','What percent saw at most three?','What percent saw more than two?','What percent saw more than three?','Which table can answer each question exactly, and is one table more correct?'),ol('At most two: (5+10+15)/60 = 50%; use the ungrouped table.','At most three: (5+10+15+12)/60 = 70%; either table works.','More than two: 1−0.50 = 50%; use the ungrouped table.','More than three: 1−0.70 = 30%; either table works.','Both tables correctly summarize the same data. Grouping makes patterns easier to see but loses detail within a class; the 2–3 group cannot separate 2 from 3.'))
PAGES['1.6']='<h2>Sampling experiment</h2>'+p('Follow the textbook’s restaurant-sampling sequence: simple random, systematic, stratified by city, stratified by price, and cluster by city. The textbook’s historical restaurant table is a sampling frame for this activity, not a current directory. Assign a distinct number to each listed restaurant before sampling.')
PAGES['1.6']+=p('Use the restaurant frame in <a href="https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/1-6-sampling-experiment">Section 1.6</a> (reproduced below). Record the actual restaurants selected as well as your method. Random samples can differ and still be valid.')+'<!-- RESTAURANT_TABLE -->'
PAGES['1.6']+=ex('1. Simple random sample',p('Choose 15 restaurants. Describe the procedure and record the selected names.'),p('Number all N restaurants in a fixed order, then select 15 distinct labels uniformly at random. Every set of 15 must have the same selection probability. Do not choose familiar restaurants or retain repeated labels as if they were new restaurants. Your selected names depend on the random draw.'))
PAGES['1.6']+=ex('2. Systematic sample',p('Choose a systematic sample of 15 restaurants. Explain how you handle a frame size not divisible by 15.'),p('For N divisible by 15, let k=N/15, choose a uniform random starting integer from 1 through k, and take every kth name. For general N, use fractional interval k=N/15: choose u uniformly in (0,k] and take positions ceil(u+jk), j=0,…,14. The spacing is fixed apart from rounding, all 15 are distinct, and no wrapping is required. Check whether the ordering has a pattern related to the variable of interest.'))
PAGES['1.6']+=ex('3. Stratify by city',p('Select about 25% of the restaurants within each city, rounding each city’s count to the nearest whole number. Record the number selected per city.'),p('Cities are strata. Count Nᵢ in each city, allocate nᵢ = round(0.25Nᵢ), then choose nᵢ distinct restaurants randomly within every city. The rounded totals should be checked against the actual frame; do not force a stated grand total if the frame counts disagree. Every city contributes to the sample.'))
PAGES['1.6']+=ex('4. Stratify by entree price',p('Repeat the 25% stratified procedure, now using the price categories rather than cities.'),p('The price bands are now strata. Pool all restaurants in each band across cities, count each band, round its quarter, and sample randomly within each band. This gives representation across price categories. Use consistent boundaries so every restaurant belongs to one stratum.'))
PAGES['1.6']+=ex('5. Cluster by city',p('Choose two cities at random and include their restaurants. Contrast this with stratifying by city.'),p('Choose two distinct cities uniformly, then include every restaurant in those cities. The number of restaurants depends on the chosen cities. Stratification samples within every city; clustering selects only some cities and takes all units in those selected clusters. Cities differ in size, so a simple unweighted restaurant average from this design may need careful interpretation.'))
PAGES['9.0']='<h2>Hypothesis testing: conceptual overview</h2>'+p('This course studies the ideas and interpretation of hypothesis testing. The sequence follows the textbook’s Sections 9.1–9.5, without the full computational test procedures. A hypothesis test compares sample evidence with what a specified population model would predict.')
PAGES['9.0']+='<h2>9.1 · Null and alternative hypotheses</h2>'+p('H₀ is the reference claim about a population parameter. Hₐ states the departure being investigated. Choose the direction before looking at the sample result. The equality boundary belongs in H₀; use ≠ for two-sided alternatives and &lt; or &gt; for one-sided alternatives.')
PAGES['9.0']+=ex('Hypotheses about a mean',p('A service claims a mean completion time of 12 minutes. Write hypotheses to investigate whether the actual mean differs from 12, is greater than 12, or is less than 12. Is the claim about μ or x̄?'),ol('Different: H₀: μ=12; Hₐ: μ≠12.','Greater: H₀ at the boundary μ=12 (or composite μ≤12); Hₐ: μ>12.','Less: H₀ at the boundary μ=12 (or composite μ≥12); Hₐ: μ<12.','The hypotheses concern μ, the population mean. The observed sample mean x̄ supplies evidence.'))
PAGES['9.0']+=ex('Hypotheses about a proportion',p('An organization claims that 60% of clients are satisfied. A researcher suspects the true fraction is lower. Write the hypotheses and identify the direction.'),p('H₀: p=0.60 at the reference boundary; Hₐ: p<0.60. This is a left-tailed test. p is the population proportion, while p̂ is the observed sample proportion.'))
PAGES['9.0']+='<h2>9.2 · Decisions and errors</h2>'+p('There are two possible decisions: reject H₀ or fail to reject H₀. A Type I error rejects a true H₀. A Type II error fails to reject a false H₀. The significance level α controls the Type I error rate under the null model. Power is the probability of rejecting H₀ for a specified alternative.')
PAGES['9.0']+=ex('Identify the two errors',p('A test asks whether the mean service time exceeds 12 minutes. Describe Type I and Type II errors in context.'),table(['Truth / decision','Reject H₀','Fail to reject H₀'],[['Null is true','Type I error','Correct decision'],['Alternative is true','Correct decision','Type II error']])+p('Type I: conclude evidence of a mean above 12 when the null is true. Type II: fail to detect a mean above 12 when it really is above 12. Failing to reject is not proof that H₀ is true.'))
PAGES['9.0']+=ex('Power and study design',p('How can a larger sample affect power? Does a smaller α automatically make every kind of error less likely?'),p('A larger well-designed sample usually reduces standard error and increases power for a fixed effect size and α. Reducing α makes rejection harder and can increase Type II error if other features stay fixed. Power also depends on effect size and variability. Larger n does not cure bias or dependence.'))
PAGES['9.0']+='<h2>9.3 · Null distributions and test statistics</h2>'+p('The null sampling distribution describes how a statistic varies if H₀ holds. A standardized test statistic measures distance from the null value in standard-error units. The appropriate reference model depends on the parameter, sampling design, known or estimated SD, and distributional conditions.')
PAGES['9.0']+=ex('Read a standardized discrepancy',p('The null mean is 12, a sample mean is 13.2, and its standard error is 0.4. What does the standardized discrepancy say? Does it alone give a probability?'),p('(13.2−12)/0.4 = 3. The sample mean is three standard errors above the null mean. This is not itself a probability. A reference distribution and a specified tail direction are needed to obtain a p-value.'))
PAGES['9.0']+='<h2>9.4 · P-values, significance, and conclusions</h2>'+p('A p-value is the probability, assuming H₀ and the model assumptions, of a test statistic at least as extreme as the observed one in the direction(s) of Hₐ. It is not the probability that H₀ is true. Compare it with the prespecified α.')
PAGES['9.0']+=ex('Make and state a decision',p('At α=0.05, one study has p-value 0.018 and another has p-value 0.14. State each decision and a suitable conclusion.'),ol('0.018≤0.05: reject H₀; the sample provides evidence for the stated alternative, subject to the model and design assumptions.','0.14>0.05: fail to reject H₀; the sample does not provide sufficient evidence for the alternative. This does not establish equivalence or prove H₀.'))
PAGES['9.0']+=ex('Interpret a p-value',p('A report says “p=0.03 means there is a 3% chance that the null hypothesis is true.” Correct it.'),p('If H₀ and the assumptions hold, the probability of evidence at least this extreme, measured by the chosen test statistic and tail(s), is 3%. The p-value does not assign a probability to the truth of H₀.'))
PAGES['9.0']+='<h2>9.5 · Reading a complete test report</h2>'+p('A useful report identifies the population parameter, hypotheses, study design, assumptions, test statistic, p-value, significance level, and contextual conclusion. Also report effect size and uncertainty; statistical significance does not establish practical importance or causation.')
PAGES['9.0']+=ex('Confidence intervals and tests',p('A 95% confidence interval for a population mean is (12.1,12.9). What would the matching two-sided α=0.05 test of H₀: μ=12 conclude? What qualification is necessary?'),p('Reject H₀, because 12 is outside the interval. This equivalence uses a matching confidence-interval/test procedure with the same assumptions and two-sided level. It should not be applied automatically to mismatched tests or to one-sided questions.'))
PAGES['9.0']+=ex('Significance and practical importance',p('A very large study finds a mean improvement of 0.02 minutes with p=0.001. What can you conclude, and what still matters?'),p('The result may be statistically significant under the model, but 0.02 minute is only 1.2 seconds. Whether that matters depends on costs, context, and uncertainty. Check design, bias, independence, multiple comparisons, and whether causal language is justified.'))
